r nucleon", "nucleosynthesis", "nuclear stability"], "role": "floater", "items": {"add": [], "remove": []}}, {"title": "Fission and Fusion", "question": "A nuclear power plant splits uranium-235; a fusion experiment joins hydrogen isotopes into helium. Both release enormous energy, and a control-room readout shows megawatts either way. How can both splitting heavy nuclei and joining light ones release energy?", "model_answer": "Per nucleon: ²³⁵U 7.62, ²³⁸U 7.60, ²³⁹Pu 7.59 MeV (below the peak on the heavy side); ³He 2.61, ³H 2.87, ⁶Li 5.37 MeV (far below on the light side). Fission moves heavy nuclei up toward the peak; fusion moves light nuclei up toward it. Either way, products are more tightly bound and the difference is released.", "target": "Both fission and fusion release energy because both move nuclei toward the peak of the binding-energy-per-nucleon curve.", "keywords": ["fission", "fusion", "toward the peak", "more tightly bound", "per nucleon", "uranium", "hydrogen isotopes", "energy released"], "liquids": ["FF1", "FF2", "FF3", "FF4", "FF5", "FF6"], "kinds": ["binding"], "fits": ["FF1", "FF2", "FF3", "FF4", "FF5", "FF6"], "media": {"image_with_question": "ch21/Fission_and_fusion.jpg", "image_after_account": "", "image_alt": "", "simulation": "ch21/binding_energy_simulator.html?nuclide={nuclide}&theme={theme}", "simulation_opens": "meaning", "credit": "AI image made by the instructor with Gemini"}, "label_words": ["nuclear fission", "nuclear fusion", "binding energy per nucleon"], "role": "floater", "items": {"add": [], "remove": []}}], "moves": {"account_given": {"state": "account_given", "stage": "opening", "label_when_done": "Particle account given", "switch_ask": "Does a nucleus weigh more or less than its separate parts? Where did the difference go?", "notebook_prompt": "I am learning nuclear binding energy. Explain the mass defect, E = mc², and binding energy per nucleon. Use a nuclide other than mine. Do not solve any problem for me. Then ask me one